#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f3436d-bc76-5261-8ad1-368ca3b77f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430915} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6b3f5a-f220-5554-9220-850158e49738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430919} {:answer/id #uuid "6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430929} {:answer/id #uuid "e4005834-c2ac-5578-8587-1d569f479bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430936} {:answer/id #uuid "e993ee67-8cbb-5ee4-a9bb-9dc04ab4bbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b730726-fed2-5b3e-8ffc-354b2c3bf2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430929} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4a0d219-93b4-5cee-98eb-dc3219549951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28a83ce5-6b28-57bd-96e8-b831ae6041a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162573" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y-z,\\,y-2x,\\,x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+4z) \\\\  & =0+1+4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b92e2ac-43e0-56d7-9ecd-75553160a01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430918} {:answer/id #uuid "41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430920} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7901d4-d410-5e21-8e33-71572db7a730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430924} {:answer/id #uuid "6aacb83d-c030-5c62-a7af-140b5f553083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430928} {:answer/id #uuid "ceca5263-40f9-5ba9-8496-31c6fead0bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e506e3-ebbe-5f8b-ae29-c18276419687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430920} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15f2475-f304-5fb8-a434-536b4a248735" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2b40e8-b423-5c7a-b959-43a67763f792" :question/math-academy-id "q-162574" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $F=(yz-2x)\\,i+3y\\,j+(x+z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+z) \\\\  & =-2+3+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $3$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} {:answer/id #uuid "06c35222-68d1-50e1-b3be-f3859d1566f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430912} {:answer/id #uuid "165ccd61-a96d-55ca-a818-a06291f0c969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430914} {:answer/id #uuid "661de3a9-92bc-5da3-91fa-17df7bbafc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430925} {:answer/id #uuid "fd490bcb-15ef-5833-9df8-21e37b4504ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069804e3-7c58-5f7e-b7b1-a0c2c5c704f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430911} :answer-field/id #uuid "89b39073-1256-589c-ae05-81365bf7916b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd56ae3f-1187-5181-af79-1866b5188a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162575" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xyz\\,j+2y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y) \\\\  & =2x+xz+0 \\\\  & =2x+xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+xz)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,2x+xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} {:answer/id #uuid "4e983312-df08-546e-9806-4e462f4e1659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430923} {:answer/id #uuid "688adcb4-8f82-5557-b006-90cbca149a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430927} {:answer/id #uuid "8128a8cf-b3f9-556e-aefb-154a9c6699fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430931} {:answer/id #uuid "d03cded5-b5e9-5895-a862-9145e719e5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,{z}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499cdd5b-e6c0-5bd4-bfc7-e7acaa3926fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ef02b27-25c8-5d51-9b89-2000755f118a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00083281-29d7-555a-802b-bb136fd26b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-162586" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=z\\,i-4y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{3}) \\\\  & =0-4+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x-z+2\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3{z}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x-z+2}\\,3{z}^{2}-4\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430913} {:answer/id #uuid "23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430916} {:answer/id #uuid "475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430921} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430935} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-162595" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430917} {:answer/id #uuid "676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430926} {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430934} {:answer/id #uuid "fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162605" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
